Field Details Purpose Check karta hai string kisi text par end hoti hai ya nahi Syntax <code>string.endswith(suffix)</code> Example <code>"photo.png".endswith(".png")</code> Output <code>True</code> When to use File type check karna (images, PDFs, etc.) Common Mistakes Tuple bhi de sakte hain: <code>endswith((".png", ".jpg"))</code> — beginners ye feature miss karte hain <pre><code>files = ["a.png", "b.txt", "c.jpg"]
for f in files:
    if f.endswith((".png", ".jpg")):
        print(f, "is an image")
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Dictionary ki saari keys return karta hai Syntax <code>dict.keys()</code> Example <code>person.keys()</code> Output <code>dict_keys(['name', 'age'])</code> When to use Loop mein keys check karne ke liye Common Mistakes Direct list treat karte hain — actually list nahi, <code>dict_keys</code> object hai <pre><code>person = {"name": "Ali", "age": 25}
print(list(person.keys()))  # ['name', 'age']
for k in person.keys():
    print(k)
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Dictionary mein naye key-value pairs add ya existing ko update karta hai Syntax <code>dict.update(other_dict)</code> Example <code>person.update({"age": 26, "city": "Lahore"})</code> Output "age" update ho jayega, "city" naya add ho jayega When to use Multiple values ek saath update/add karne ke liye Common Mistakes Existing keys ko silently overwrite kar deta hai — data loss ka risk <pre><code>person = {"name": "Ali", "age": 25}
person.update({"age": 26, "city": "Lahore"})
print(person)  # {'name': 'Ali', 'age': 26, 'city': 'Lahore'}
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Key ke basis par item hata kar uski value return karta hai Syntax <code>dict.pop(key, default)</code> Example <code>person.pop("age")</code> Output Age ki value, aur dict se "age" remove ho jata hai When to use Key nikaal kar use karna ho, phir dict se hatana ho Common Mistakes Default value na do aur key na mile to <code>KeyError</code> aayega <pre><code>person = {"name": "Ali", "age": 25}
age = person.pop("age")
print(age)     # 25
print(person)  # {'name': 'Ali'}
print(person.pop("city", "Not Found"))  # 'Not Found'
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Last inserted key-value pair ko remove karke return karta hai Syntax <code>dict.popitem()</code> Example <code>person.popitem()</code> Output <code>('age', 25)</code> type tuple When to use Stack-jaisa behavior chahiye ho (LIFO) Common Mistakes Empty dictionary par call karne se <code>KeyError</code> aata hai <pre><code>person = {"name": "Ali", "age": 25}
print(person.popitem())  # ('age', 25)
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Key exist karti hai to uski value return karta hai, warna naya key-value set karta hai Syntax <code>dict.setdefault(key, default)</code> Example <code>person.setdefault("city", "Unknown")</code> Output Agar "city" nahi hai to add ho jati hai with value "Unknown" When to use Counting/grouping logic mein bohot useful Common Mistakes <code>get()</code> se confuse hote hain — <code>setdefault()</code> dictionary ko modify bhi karta hai <pre><code>word_count = {}
words = ["a", "b", "a", "c", "b", "a"]
for w in words:
    word_count.setdefault(w, 0)
    word_count[w] += 1
print(word_count)  # {'a': 3, 'b': 2, 'c': 1}
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Dictionary ke saare items hata deta hai Syntax <code>dict.clear()</code> Example <code>person.clear()</code> Output <code>{}</code> When to use Dictionary reset karne ke liye Common Mistakes Same object empty hota hai, naya nahi banta <pre><code>person = {"name": "Ali"}
person.clear()
print(person)  # {}
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Dictionary ki shallow copy banata hai Syntax <code>dict.copy()</code> Example <code>new_dict = old_dict.copy()</code> Output Independent copy (top-level) When to use Original dictionary ko safe rakhte hue changes karni hon Common Mistakes <code>new_dict = old_dict</code> reference banata hai, copy nahi <pre><code>a = {"x": 1}
b = a.copy()
b["x"] = 99
print(a)  # {'x': 1}
print(b)  # {'x': 99}
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Set mein ek naya element add karta hai Syntax <code>set.add(element)</code> Example <code>my_set.add(5)</code> Output 5 set mein add ho jata hai (agar pehle se nahi hai) When to use Unique items collect karne ke liye Common Mistakes Element pehle se hai to silently ignore ho jata hai, error nahi aata <pre><code>s = {1, 2, 3}
s.add(4)
print(s)  # {1, 2, 3, 4}
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Set se element hata deta hai Syntax <code>set.remove(element)</code> Example <code>s.remove(2)</code> Output 2 set se hat jata hai When to use Jab pakka pata ho element maujood hai Common Mistakes Element na hone par <code>KeyError</code> aata hai <pre><code>s = {1, 2, 3}
s.remove(2)
print(s)  # {1, 3}
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose <code>remove()</code> jaisa hi hai lekin element na milne par error nahi deta Syntax <code>set.discard(element)</code> Example <code>s.discard(10)</code> Output Kuch nahi hota, silently skip When to use Safe removal ke liye jab unsure ho Common Mistakes Beginners <code>remove()</code> use karte reh jaate hain aur crash face karte hain <pre><code>s = {1, 2, 3}
s.discard(10)  # no error
print(s)  # {1, 2, 3}
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Do sets ke saare unique elements combine karta hai Syntax <code>set1.union(set2)</code> or <code>set1 \| set2</code> Example <code>{1,2}.union({2,3})</code> Output <code>{1, 2, 3}</code> When to use Do groups merge karne ke liye bina duplicates ke Common Mistakes Original sets change nahi hote, naya set return hota hai <pre><code>a = {1, 2, 3}
b = {3, 4, 5}
print(a.union(b))  # {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Dono sets mein common elements return karta hai Syntax <code>set1.intersection(set2)</code> or <code>set1 &amp; set2</code> Example <code>{1,2,3}.intersection({2,3,4})</code> Output <code>{2, 3}</code> When to use Common items find karne ke liye (jaise mutual friends) Common Mistakes <code>&amp;</code> ko <code>and</code> samajh lena — alag cheezein hain <pre><code>a = {1, 2, 3}
b = {2, 3, 4}
print(a.intersection(b))  # {2, 3}
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Ek set mein hain lekin doosre mein nahi, wo elements return karta hai Syntax <code>set1.difference(set2)</code> or <code>set1 - set2</code> Example <code>{1,2,3}.difference({2,3})</code> Output <code>{1}</code> When to use Ek group mein kya extra hai doosre ke comparison mein Common Mistakes Order matter karta hai: <code>a - b</code> != <code>b - a</code> <pre><code>a = {1, 2, 3}
b = {2, 3, 4}
print(a.difference(b))  # {1}
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Kisi variable ka data type batata hai Syntax <code>type(object)</code> Example <code>type(5)</code> Output <code>&lt;class 'int'&gt;</code> When to use Debugging ke liye, ya type check karne ke liye Common Mistakes <code>type()</code> ko comparison mein use karna: <code>if type(x) == int</code> — behtar hai <code>isinstance()</code> use karo (inheritance handle karta hai) <pre><code>print(type(5))        # &lt;class 'int'&gt;
print(type("hi"))      # &lt;class 'str'&gt;
print(type([1,2]))     # &lt;class 'list'&gt;
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Object ke <code>__dict__</code> (attributes) ko dictionary form mein deta hai Syntax <code>vars(object)</code> Example <code>vars(my_object)</code> Output <code>{'name': 'Ali', 'age': 25}</code> type dictionary When to use Class instances ke attributes debug karne ke liye Common Mistakes Sab objects ke saath kaam nahi karta (jaise built-in types ke saath error de sakta hai) <pre><code>class Person:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

p = Person("Ali")
print(vars(p))  # {'name': 'Ali'}
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Condition ke basis par decision lena Syntax <code>if condition: ... elif condition2: ... else: ...</code> Example <code>if age &gt;= 18: print("Adult")</code> Output Condition ke hisaab se output When to use Har jagah jahan decision-making chahiye Common Mistakes <code>=</code> (assignment) aur <code>==</code> (comparison) mix karna. <code>elif</code> ki jagah <code>else if</code> likhna (Python mein sirf <code>elif</code> valid hai) <pre><code>age = 20
if age &lt; 13:
    print("Child")
elif age &lt; 18:
    print("Teenager")
else:
    print("Adult")
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Ek class doosri class ki properties/methods inherit (le) sakti hai Syntax <code>class Child(Parent):</code> Example <code>class Dog(Animal):</code> Output Child class parent ke saare methods use kar sakti hai When to use Jab code reuse karna ho aur "is-a" relationship ho (Dog is an Animal) Common Mistakes Parent ka <code>__init__</code> call karna bhool jaana (super() use karke) <pre><code>class Animal:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

    def speak(self):
        print(f"{self.name} makes a sound")

class Dog(Animal):
    def speak(self):
        print(f"{self.name} barks")

d = Dog("Rex")
d.speak()  # Rex barks
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Parent class ke methods/constructor ko call karta hai Syntax <code>super().__init__(args)</code> Example <code>super().__init__(name)</code> Output Parent ka <code>__init__</code> bhi run ho jata hai When to use Jab child class parent ka logic reuse karna chahe, plus apna extra logic add kare Common Mistakes <code>super()</code> use na karke parent ka <code>__init__</code> overwrite kar dena bina call kiye <pre><code>class Animal:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

class Dog(Animal):
    def __init__(self, name, breed):
        super().__init__(name)  # parent's __init__ call
        self.breed = breed

d = Dog("Rex", "Labrador")
print(d.name, d.breed)  # Rex Labrador
</code></pre>

Field Details Purpose Khud se ek exception generate/throw karta hai Syntax <code>raise ExceptionType("message")</code> Example <code>raise ValueError("Invalid input")</code> Output Program us jagah exception raise kar deta hai When to use Jab custom validation fail ho aur caller ko batana ho Common Mistakes Generic <code>Exception</code> raise karna jab specific type (jaise <code>ValueError</code>, <code>TypeError</code>) zyada meaningful hota <pre><code>def set_age(age):
    if age &lt; 0:
        raise ValueError("Age cannot be negative")
    return age

try:
    set_age(-5)
except ValueError as e:
    print(e)  # Age cannot be negative
</code></pre>
